  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Test whether basal migration of mesoderm cell nuclei during early Drosophila gastrulation acts as an intracellular spatial organizer that redistributes RhoGEF2 and the cortical actomyosin network to generate a lateral actomyosin–E-cadherin junctional tier coordinating epithelial folding with convergent extension.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A coordinated morphogenetic mechanism can be investigated by combining evidence on two-tier actomyosin organization, nuclear-position dynamics, and unresolved cytoskeletal control."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) The dataset reports mean values with 95% confidence intervals and includes temporal cross-correlation between nuclear speed and MyoII recruitment."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) The plotted data relate mean nuclear position to mean apical cell surface and include an exponential fit to raw values."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Time-lapse and time-dependent measurements document nuclear-position changes alongside MyoII lateral intensity during apical constriction."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Time-dependent MyoII and nuclear-position data support analysis of the temporal relationship between nuclear dynamics and MyoII recruitment."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Because organelle positioning can contribute to cell remodeling, the observed temporal relationship between nuclear dynamics and MyoII recruitment motivates the hypothesis that nuclear position may participate in cytoskeletal remodeling."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) Investigating the elusive role of nuclear position during mesoderm folding can address whether nuclear positioning contributes to cytoskeletal-network organization."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) Simultaneous epithelial folding and extension during gastrulation exemplifies the broader unresolved problem of how composite morphogenesis is coordinated."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) The prospective mesoderm studied during early Drosophila gastrulation is an appropriate context for examining concomitant folding and extension as composite morphogenesis."];
  "R20" [label="(0,0,0) Sagittal imaging supports the presence of a two-tier MyoII organization in mesoderm cells, consistent with distinct junctional tiers tailored for different functions."];
  "R21" [label="(0,0,0) The statistical and temporal analyses together characterize how nuclear dynamics and MyoII recruitment are related over time."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) Time-lapse observations show apical-constriction-associated nuclear displacement and provide the basis for measuring nuclear-position changes."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) A testable explanation is that changes in nuclear position may help organize cytoskeletal components involved in formation of the second junctional tier."];
  "R24" [label="(0,0,0) The unresolved role of nuclear position and the aim to identify coordinating mechanisms motivate investigation of the nucleus during mesoderm folding."];
  "R25" [label="(0,0,0) The study is framed to test whether nuclear-position-dependent cytoskeletal organization helps coordinate mesoderm folding with tissue extension."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) The laterally positioned second junctional tier is associated with initiation of cell intercalation as part of the mesoderm two-tier organization."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Second-tier formation involves coalescence of the lateral actomyosin network within a broader genetically patterned two-tier junctional system."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Nuclear position can be quantified over time when nuclear-position measurements are represented using an apex-to-nucleus positional measure."];
